from h2 import *
A = dict(id="A", levels=L2, kind="Web page", source="Fenland Rural Housing Advice", numbered=True, title="Moving to a village: what to consider", body=[
 {"p": "Many people dream of swapping the city for a village, with its open views, quiet lanes and friendly faces. Village life can be wonderful, but it works best for those who have thought carefully about what they are choosing. A little research now will help you avoid disappointment later."},
 {"p": "Think first about getting around. Many villages have only a few buses each day, and some have none at all in the evening, so a car is often essential. If you do not drive, check the timetable before you commit, because a short journey can turn into a bit of a trek."},
 {"p": "Next, look at local services. A village shop with a post office, a primary school and a doctor's surgery can make daily life much easier, but each of these is hard to keep open when the population is small. Find out which facilities are within walking distance and which are a drive away."},
 {"p": "Housing is the next big question. Homes in villages are often older, with larger gardens, and they can cost more than similar properties in towns because so few come up for sale. Rented homes are scarce, so you may have to wait. Ask the parish council whether any new houses are planned."},
 {"p": "Community matters too. Villages thrive when people take part, whether by helping at the summer fair, joining the church committee or simply popping into the pub. Why not go along to an event before you move? You will soon get a feel for the place."},
 {"p": "Finally, visit at different times of year. A village in June is a picture postcard, but in January it can feel very different. Spend a weekend there in winter if you can, and make sure you are happy with what you find. If you are, you're all set."}])
B = dict(id="B", levels=L2, kind="Magazine article", source="Rural Review magazine", title="Why are young people leaving our villages?", body=[
 {"p": "Walk through almost any English village on a weekday afternoon and you will notice who is missing. A survey of 2,500 people aged 18 to 30 by the Rural Futures Group found that 57 per cent of those raised in villages had moved away by the age of 25, and only one in eight of them expected to return."},
 {"p": "The reasons are not mysterious. House prices in villages are often far higher than local wages can support, and homes to rent are rare. For many young people, a village home is not so much the first rung of the ladder as \"a ladder with the bottom rungs missing\". Others leave for work, since jobs in the countryside tend to be scarce, seasonal or poorly paid."},
 {"p": "Transport is another barrier. Without a car, a young person who lives in a village may depend on a bus that runs twice a day, which makes college, evening jobs and friendships difficult to sustain. Some say the situation leaves them feeling cut off, and no wonder."},
 {"p": "The effect on the villages themselves can be severe. As young families leave, schools lose pupils and shops lose customers. Eventually, the village risks \"a hollowing out\": the buildings remain, but the life drains away. Rural pubs, post offices and clubs are closing at a worrying rate."},
 {"box": "**Advice from the Rural Communities Forum:** Parish councils should survey local young people about what they need. Landowners should consider selling small plots for affordable homes. Bus operators should trial evening and weekend routes. And villagers should welcome newcomers, whatever their age."},
 {"p": "There are encouraging examples. In one village, a community land trust has built eight low-cost homes, all reserved for local people, and the primary school has since gained a dozen pupils. Residents say that the change has brought new energy to the place."},
 {"p": "In my view, a village without young people is a village without a future, and it is the duty of everyone who lives there to do something about it. Waiting for someone else to act is not an option."}])
C = dict(id="C", levels=L2, kind="Transcript of a speech", source="Parish council meeting", title="Speech to a parish council meeting", body=[
 {"p": "Good evening, everyone, and thank you for coming to what I know is a difficult discussion. I have lived in this village for forty years, I have served on this council for twelve, and I am asking you tonight to say yes to the new homes on Mill Field."},
 {"p": "Let me begin with a story. Last spring, a young woman I know, born and raised in this parish, told me she was leaving. She had a job at the surgery, a family nearby and a love for this place, but she could not find a home she could afford. When she left, we did not just lose a neighbour. We lost a future."},
 {"p": "So what do we need? First, we need homes that local people can afford. Second, we need young families, because without them the school will close. Third, we need to keep our shop, our hall and our pub alive, and they can only survive if people live here all year round."},
 {"p": "Will there be disruption? Of course there will. Will some of you miss the view across the field? I will, too. But what is the alternative? A village of empty houses, shuttered windows and a bus stop that nobody uses. I refuse to let that happen on my watch."},
 {"p": "The figures speak for themselves. Eighteen of the twenty-four new homes will be reserved for people with a local connection, and the developer has promised to pay for a new footpath, a play area and a bus shelter. Since our school roll fell by a third in five years, we cannot afford to hesitate."},
 {"p": "I have heard the worry about traffic. It is a fair worry, and I share it. That is why I have asked for a speed limit on the lane and a crossing by the school. We can protect our village and still make room for the next generation."},
 {"p": "Think of the Saturday fair in ten years' time. I picture young families with pushchairs, children running between the stalls and every volunteer wearing a smile. That is the village I want to leave behind, and I believe it is the village you want, too."},
 {"p": "It will be difficult, but it will be worth it. Please vote with your hearts, and vote for the future. Thank you."}])

